data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1407,"item_id":"fc_031097c1e2840610016adb39332edc8191a55064ec1c02ded5","output_index":2,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_input\\\":\\\"prior-candidate.json\\\",\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix r>0, J=[r/2,3r/2], and choose compact C from H003. For every s∈J, u(·,s)=q on the open complement of C. Smoothness implies every first and higher derivative in x and t vanishes there (at endpoint times use continuity from the interior of J); consequently e and e_t, at each fixed s∈J, are continuous and supported in C. A continuous function supported in a compact set has compact support. On ℝⁿ with its Euclidean topology, Borel measurable opens, Lebesgue measure finite on compact sets, and scalar normed additive group, M001 therefore gives integrability of e(·,s), e_t(·,s), and every continuous compactly supported scalar field used below. In particular E(s) is the ordinary finite Lebesgue integral, not merely an uninterpreted totalized value. The set C×J is compact by M005, instantiating its two topological spaces as ℝⁿ and ℝ and its sets as C and J; e_t is continuous there. M004 then gives uniform continuity of e_t on C×J. To apply the general differentiation-under-integral theorem M006, put α=ℝⁿ with its Borel measurable space, μ=Lebesgue measure, 𝕜=ℝ, E=ℝ, x₀=r, s=(r/2,3r/2), and F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ), F'(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). The neighborhood condition holds since r is interior to s; all F(τ) are continuous and compactly supported in C, hence strongly measurable, and F(r) is integrable by M001. F'(r) is strongly measurable. Uniform continuity on C×J gives a finite bound K for |e_t(x,τ)| on C×J: cover compact C×J by finitely many continuity neighborhoods, or use the compact-image boundedness of a continuous real function. Set b(x)=K·1_C(x). It is integrable because C is measurable and has finite Lebesgue measure. For x∉C the derivative is zero; for x∈C the bound is K, so |F'(τ)(x)|≤b(x) for every τ∈s. Smoothness gives HasDerivAt (τ↦e(x,τ)) (e_t(x,τ)) τ for every x,τ. These verify each hypothesis of M006, yielding E differentiable at r and E'(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. This use of M006 proves integrability of the derivative before its integral is interpreted. The compact set C itself is contained in a bounded coordinate box: each continuous coordinate projection of C is compact in ℝ, hence bounded; because n is a positive finite integer, choose L larger than all finitely many coordinate bounds. This provides a single box [-L,L]^n containing C. The product of this box and J is compact by M005. These compactness statements are used explicitly, not assumed by the declarations of local objects.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"At fixed r, for each j define g_j(x)=u_t(x,r)·u_{x_j}(x,r). By the first paragraph both factors and their derivatives vanish outside C; g_j and ∂_j g_j are smooth and supported in C, and M001 gives integrability. Product differentiation in the m Euclidean coordinates gives ∂_j g_j=(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}+u_t·u_{x_jx_j}; the dot product is exactly the finite sum over m components. For each fixed choice y of the other n−1 coordinates, the function z↦g_j(x(y,z)) is smooth and vanishes for |z|>L. M002, instantiated with V=ℝ, endpoints −L,L, f(z)=g_j(x(y,z)) and f'(z)=∂_j g_j(x(y,z)), applies: ℝ is complete, the derivative identity follows from smoothness, and f' is interval-integrable because it is continuous on the compact interval. The endpoint values are zero, so its interval integral is zero; it vanishes outside that interval, hence its whole-line integral is zero. The case n=1 has the other-coordinate factor ℝ⁰, a singleton, so the same statement is the ordinary one-dimensional integral. Product integrability of the coordinate-permuted ∂_j g_j is established before invoking M003: it is continuous and compactly supported after the Euclidean coordinate permutation, so M001 applies with X=ℝⁿ, V=ℝ and Lebesgue measure. The coordinate permutation preserves Lebesgue measure because it permutes an orthonormal coordinate basis; Lebesgue volume of boxes is the product of side lengths, unchanged under this permutation, and boxes generate the Borel measure. Both factor Lebesgue measures are σ-finite and therefore S-finite; ℝ is a real normed space. Thus every hypothesis of M003 is met, and Fubini identifies the whole-space integral with the iterated integral, which is zero by the preceding one-variable calculation. Hence ∫∂_j g_j=0 and ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}=−∫u_t·u_{x_jx_j}. All displayed whole-space integrals are of continuous compactly supported fields and are integrable by M001. Differentiating the exact Frobenius definition e=|u_t|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|u_{x_j}|² yields e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_{x_j}·∂_j u_t; finite sums commute with the integral. The preceding identities and Δ_xu=Σ_j u_{x_jx_j} give E'(r)=2∫u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu). Differentiating H001 gives 2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. At each point H002 supplies a scalar a with u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a u; taking its inner product with u_t gives u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0. This integrand is identically zero and integrable, so E'(r)=0. No regularity of a is invoked. Since r was arbitrary positive, the conclusion holds at every interior time.\\\"}]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","sequence_number":1408,"output_index":2,"item":{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_031097c1e2840610016adb39332edc8191a55064ec1c02ded5","call_id":"call_1c7a9fd9464a4498984bc4ec7ba8102b","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_input\\\":\\\"prior-candidate.json\\\",\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/0/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Fix r>0, J=[r/2,3r/2], and choose compact C from H003. For every s∈J, u(·,s)=q on the open complement of C. Smoothness implies every first and higher derivative in x and t vanishes there (at endpoint times use continuity from the interior of J); consequently e and e_t, at each fixed s∈J, are continuous and supported in C. A continuous function supported in a compact set has compact support. On ℝⁿ with its Euclidean topology, Borel measurable opens, Lebesgue measure finite on compact sets, and scalar normed additive group, M001 therefore gives integrability of e(·,s), e_t(·,s), and every continuous compactly supported scalar field used below. In particular E(s) is the ordinary finite Lebesgue integral, not merely an uninterpreted totalized value. The set C×J is compact by M005, instantiating its two topological spaces as ℝⁿ and ℝ and its sets as C and J; e_t is continuous there. M004 then gives uniform continuity of e_t on C×J. To apply the general differentiation-under-integral theorem M006, put α=ℝⁿ with its Borel measurable space, μ=Lebesgue measure, 𝕜=ℝ, E=ℝ, x₀=r, s=(r/2,3r/2), and F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ), F'(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ). The neighborhood condition holds since r is interior to s; all F(τ) are continuous and compactly supported in C, hence strongly measurable, and F(r) is integrable by M001. F'(r) is strongly measurable. Uniform continuity on C×J gives a finite bound K for |e_t(x,τ)| on C×J: cover compact C×J by finitely many continuity neighborhoods, or use the compact-image boundedness of a continuous real function. Set b(x)=K·1_C(x). It is integrable because C is measurable and has finite Lebesgue measure. For x∉C the derivative is zero; for x∈C the bound is K, so |F'(τ)(x)|≤b(x) for every τ∈s. Smoothness gives HasDerivAt (τ↦e(x,τ)) (e_t(x,τ)) τ for every x,τ. These verify each hypothesis of M006, yielding E differentiable at r and E'(r)=∫e_t(x,r)dx. This use of M006 proves integrability of the derivative before its integral is interpreted. The compact set C itself is contained in a bounded coordinate box: each continuous coordinate projection of C is compact in ℝ, hence bounded; because n is a positive finite integer, choose L larger than all finitely many coordinate bounds. This provides a single box [-L,L]^n containing C. The product of this box and J is compact by M005. These compactness statements are used explicitly, not assumed by the declarations of local objects.\\\"},{\\\"op\\\":\\\"replace\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/0/argument/1/text\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"At fixed r, for each j define g_j(x)=u_t(x,r)·u_{x_j}(x,r). By the first paragraph both factors and their derivatives vanish outside C; g_j and ∂_j g_j are smooth and supported in C, and M001 gives integrability. Product differentiation in the m Euclidean coordinates gives ∂_j g_j=(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}+u_t·u_{x_jx_j}; the dot product is exactly the finite sum over m components. For each fixed choice y of the other n−1 coordinates, the function z↦g_j(x(y,z)) is smooth and vanishes for |z|>L. M002, instantiated with V=ℝ, endpoints −L,L, f(z)=g_j(x(y,z)) and f'(z)=∂_j g_j(x(y,z)), applies: ℝ is complete, the derivative identity follows from smoothness, and f' is interval-integrable because it is continuous on the compact interval. The endpoint values are zero, so its interval integral is zero; it vanishes outside that interval, hence its whole-line integral is zero. The case n=1 has the other-coordinate factor ℝ⁰, a singleton, so the same statement is the ordinary one-dimensional integral. Product integrability of the coordinate-permuted ∂_j g_j is established before invoking M003: it is continuous and compactly supported after the Euclidean coordinate permutation, so M001 applies with X=ℝⁿ, V=ℝ and Lebesgue measure. The coordinate permutation preserves Lebesgue measure because it permutes an orthonormal coordinate basis; Lebesgue volume of boxes is the product of side lengths, unchanged under this permutation, and boxes generate the Borel measure. Both factor Lebesgue measures are σ-finite and therefore S-finite; ℝ is a real normed space. Thus every hypothesis of M003 is met, and Fubini identifies the whole-space integral with the iterated integral, which is zero by the preceding one-variable calculation. Hence ∫∂_j g_j=0 and ∫(∂_j u_t)·u_{x_j}=−∫u_t·u_{x_jx_j}. All displayed whole-space integrals are of continuous compactly supported fields and are integrable by M001. Differentiating the exact Frobenius definition e=|u_t|²+Σ_{j=1}^n|u_{x_j}|² yields e_t=2u_t·u_{tt}+2Σ_j u_{x_j}·∂_j u_t; finite sums commute with the integral. The preceding identities and Δ_xu=Σ_j u_{x_jx_j} give E'(r)=2∫u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu). Differentiating H001 gives 2u·u_t=0, hence u·u_t=0. At each point H002 supplies a scalar a with u_{tt}−Δ_xu=a u; taking its inner product with u_t gives u_t·(u_{tt}−Δ_xu)=a(u_t·u)=0. This integrand is identically zero and integrable, so E'(r)=0. No regularity of a is invoked. Since r was arbitrary positive, the conclusion holds at every interior time.\\\"}]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

